1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,200
Every machine learning engineer knows this loop.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:08,000
Look at the data. Build a feature. Train. Evaluate.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Realize you were wrong. Do it again.

4
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,000
It's the biggest time sink in recommender work — and it's just iteration.

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,500
So we built an agent that runs that loop by itself.

6
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:24,000
It reads the problem, reproduces the official baseline,

7
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:29,500
then proposes its own hypotheses, writes the code, trains,

8
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:33,000
and scores itself on the official metric.

9
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Seventeen iterations. Zero lines of human code.

10
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,000
The agent drives four components.

11
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:43,500
A feature builder that computes every statistic strictly from the past.

12
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:48,500
A model layer — gradient boosting, a factorization machine, item-item models.

13
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:51,500
The official evaluator, which we never modify.

14
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:55,000
And a reflection step that picks the next hypothesis.

15
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Here's a full run. The agent starts from raw KuaiRand-Pure logs

16
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,500
and builds its feature cache.

17
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:09,000
Every aggregate for a training row uses only strictly earlier days —

18
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:13,000
that discipline alone was our single biggest gain.

19
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Now it trains and scores itself with the organizers' evaluator.

20
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Watch the log: each iteration records a hypothesis, the reason,

21
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
the code change, and the validation score.

22
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,000
This is the moment that matters.

23
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:38,000
The agent adds collaborative-filtering features and the score collapses.

24
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:44,000
It inspects feature importance, sees one feature with four times the gain

25
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:47,500
of anything else, and diagnoses a leak —

26
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:51,000
the user's own history was scoring the user.

27
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:55,000
It derives the correction, reruns, and recovers. No human touched that.

28
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:00,000
It keeps going. It fans out parallel sweeps.

29
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,000
It tests a sequence model, a metric-aware weighting scheme,

30
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:08,500
and a freshness experiment —

31
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:12,500
and records all three as failures, because they were.

32
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:17,000
Finally it declares convergence under the rule it fixed in advance,

33
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:20,000
names its best checkpoint, and scores the hidden test set exactly once.

34
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:25,000
The official factorization-machine baseline scores 0.5946 on the hidden test.

35
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:28,500
We reach 0.6015. That's plus 0.0069 —

36
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:32,000
and on this benchmark the ceiling is not one point zero.

37
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:38,000
A perfect ranking scores 0.8645, because a quarter of users
have no positive label at all.

38
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,500
Against that real range, we move the baseline's share
from 31 percent to 32.4.

39
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:45,000
All of it on eight CPU cores, in four and a half hours.

40
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,000
Nothing here is specific to one dataset.

41
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:54,000
The loop, the leak detection, the past-only discipline,

42
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:56,500
and the honest logging of what failed —

43
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:59,000
that's a template for any ranking problem.

44
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Thank you.
